## Step 2. Measure the cut and the staging policy

The first run through runTixCuda reproduced the 1.412 ms inside-attention baseline. Script 02 compared moving the score to its own grid with keeping score, shift, exp and normalization in one row kernel. Moving score cost 1.620 ms. Cutting at the divided output contraction instead took 1.100 ms in the pilot. The planner now recognizes a matrix read indexed first by a reduction binder and second by a trailing output axis. It records the cut before computing inter-kernel liveness.

Script 01's pilot staged arithmetic in a row scalar with one ordinary binder. Computing the inside-layernorm terms in parallel paid off. Computing the corpus variance's square in parallel took 0.198 ms versus 0.199 ms serial, within the brief's noise band. I kept the serial path for cheap operations. Inspection also showed that storing optional terms in global scratch could grow a broadcast reduction's memory footprint with the product of its extents. The launch now declines staging if the terms would displace existing shared rows. Review the two barriers around the shared buffer and the ordinary scalar body in the fallback.

The first git add returned “Read-only file system” for the worktree index.lock. Retrying with workspace approval created design commit b149a36 before any code edits. The first detached tmux timing job lost its server on shell exit. Sharing the replacement server between jobs also interrupted some measurements when its owning shell exited. Script 04 gives each job its own server and keeps the owning shell alive. The partial logs are retained with “interrupted” in their names.
EOF
